the thing i love most about being a  mother is seeing an incredible human  that i played a role in molding and just  giving her the tools to be her best most  authentic self  my name is cheyenne rogers and i am 26  years old i am in hartford connecticut  at uconn school of law and i am in my 1l  studying to be a lawyer  [Music]  i was born to two drug addicted parents  in san pedro california  and  they lost custody of me and my siblings  shortly after my first birthday from  there we kind of bounced around a bit  from foster home to foster home in l.a  county and then i was eventually adopted  when i was six everything was fine  initially but progressively  the home just got more abusive  physically emotionally and mentally and  i eventually left at 13.  i was homeless for about a year and i  re-entered foster care and i was placed  in a group home in south central and  then when i got pregnant i was placed in  another group home by macarthur park  near echo park in la  when i got pregnant with layla it just  really encouraged me to stabilize and go  back to school and focus on her future i  needed to do good in order for her to do  good  i lived through foster care and it would  have been incredible to have had someone  that looked like me that lived life like  me and was in foster care representing  me and that's why i want to become an  attorney  i think i've always known that i wanted  to be an attorney they had so much  control over what happened to my life i  also just so i felt like i had a knack  for arguing and being an advocate and  being a lawyer is just like the next  step up for me  [Music]  i hope to eventually work with foster  youth in some capacity either  on an individual level or on a policy  level i want to end the  intergenerational cycle of foster care i  don't want my daughter to ever enter  foster care or have to experience the  child welfare system in any capacity  my daughter layla is 10 years old she's  so sassy and brave and beautiful  i get to relive my childhood through her  a better more wholesome version of what  my childhood should have been  she always feels safe she always feels  loved she always feels like there's food  in the fridge for her and knowing that  she gets to be her truest self without  fear without survival instinct just  being at the forefront  i moved out here  beginning of august and i cross country  road tripped it with my daughter and a  friend of mine so it took us about nine  days to get here  the program is three years and i will  graduate in 2024  and when i do i will have my jd my juris  doctor degree  and after i pass the bar i will be able  to say that i am cheyenne rogers esquire  hearing cheyenne rogers esquire anywhere  near my name is really kind of  mind-blowing i i never thought  i would actually get to this point so  i'm i'm excited  i think with law school  everyone's really stressed out but being  from a background where  no one  graduated college or pursued graduate  school there's this added layer of  feeling  not good enough and there's this very  real imposter syndrome  financially it's really expensive to go  to grad school  or law school i've really relied on  scholarships and financial aid to get  here  even the financial burden is just one  part of it there's also the success of  even being here only three to four  percent of foster youth will even  graduate college and the number does not  exist for graduate studies so i feel  very much like i'm exploring unknown  territory here  [Music]  hi shane how you doing i'm good how are  you  i am doing great this is so exciting  because i heard your story and i'm so  wild about all of it my mentors are choa  and i am a content creator at buzzfeed  tasty thank you for sharing your story  and your experiences with us since  you're working so hard to make a  difference we want to give you a little  something to help you out throughout  your journey zell is sending you three  thousand dollars oh my gosh you can  continue all the amazing work you're  doing uh and thanks to zell you will  have the money in a few minutes oh my  god didn't wear my waterproof mascara  we really hope you enjoyed this gift  from zell and best of luck  i'm just really blown away this  opportunity was incredible uh and i'm so  happy that  buzzfeed and zell got together and  thought that i was worthy to be selected  for this so i'm just extremely grateful  thank you so much zell i really  appreciate it
 

